Grade 1 hamstring strain from sudden acceleration during community sport to posterior thigh
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal posterior thigh tenderness, Pain on resisted knee flexion, Sudden acceleration injury mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-006
Reason for Visit: Acute severe posterior thigh pain limiting ambulation, requiring urgent assessment for complete tendon avulsion or significant structural tear.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute severe muscular pain preventing normal weight-bearing following a high-velocity injury. Urgent assessment was required to exclude complete proximal tendon avulsion and deep vein thrombosis before the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I went for a sprint, felt a sharp pull in the back of my thigh, and my leg buckled under me.
History of Present Illness: The injury occurred with an acute onset of a localized pulling sensation and mild cramping in the posterior thigh during a burst of rapid acceleration. Pain was immediate and functionally limiting. The patient describes focal tenderness deep in the proximal musculotendinous junction. The pain is exacerbated by bending the knee against resistance and provoked by passive straight leg raising. Despite the pain, the patient maintains the ability to walk independently, though sprinting or running is entirely restricted by voluntary guarding. There is no history of direct trauma to the site.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently with a pronounced antalgic gait.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired mobility restricts ability to operate heavy machinery or safely climb household stairs.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/76 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly grimacing during weight transfer onto the affected leg.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination reveals focal posterior thigh tenderness centered over the mid-substance of the biceps femoris. Pain is reliably reproduced on resisted active knee flexion. A passive straight leg raise stretch provokes mild discomfort without neurogenic radicular signs. There is preservation of normal muscle strength despite mild pain inhibition. Complete absence of a palpable structural defect, divot, or mass in the muscle belly. Lack of significant acute swelling or ecchymosis. Normal lower limb neurological examination with no sciatic nerve irritation.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting on a low sofa requiring significant core effort to rise; no obvious fall hazards noted.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine prescription medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 hamstring strain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Proximal hamstring avulsion - Ruled out due to: Absence of a palpable severe defect at the ischial tuberosity and preserved active knee flexion strength.

Sciatic radiculopathy - Ruled out due to: Normal dermatomal sensation, preserved deep tendon reflexes, and a negative straight leg raise for dural tension.

Deep vein thrombosis - Ruled out due to: Pain strictly localized to the posterior thigh following a sudden dynamic acceleration mechanism, with normal calf compartments.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden, high-velocity mechanism of injury and immediate functional decline raised suspicion for a high-grade muscle rupture or tendon avulsion. On-site assessment safely excluded severe structural compromise, confirming an isolated Grade 1 hamstring strain with intact mechanical continuity. This urgent after-hours evaluation was essential to rule out surgical emergencies, provide immediate pain management, and institute early safe mobilization parameters to prevent contracture, safely bridging the patient to routine daytime care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommended oral NSAIDs with food for 3-5 days. Instructed on the implementation of the POLICE protocol (Protection, Optimal Loading, Ice, Compression, Elevation).
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply ice and localized compression to the thigh to reduce localized tissue edema, directly resulting in decreased pain and improved walking tolerance over the next 48 hours. Use a shortened stride to avoid mechanical strain on the healing fibers.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular thigh compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 to 5 days for physiotherapy referral and ongoing rehabilitation planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department if you develop rapidly expanding bruising, severe swelling in the calf or foot, or completely lose the ability to bear weight.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending clinical progression; deep intramuscular bleeding can manifest as delayed bruising.
Clinical Course: Acute pain typically peaks at 24-48 hours. Functional return to basic activities takes 1 to 2 weeks, while returning to sprinting may require 4 to 6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate care if you develop sudden calf swelling, shortness of breath, or if your entire leg becomes cold and pale.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Early optimal loading with a normal walking gait prevents scar tissue from forming incorrectly, preserving muscle flexibility.
Actionable Steps: Walk with a shorter stride to limit pain. Do not passively stretch the hamstring forcefully. Apply ice to the back of the thigh for 15 minutes after mobilizing.

